      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Build Your Online Presence</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Create your website and social media presence before launch. Your website should showcase your brand story, products, and make purchasing easy. Ecommerce platforms like Shopify, WooCommerce, or BigCommerce provide robust solutions. Secure your domain name and set up professional email addresses. Social media presence on platforms where your target customer spends time builds community and awareness. Consistent visual identity across all touchpoints builds recognition.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Develop Brand Content and Assets</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Create professional photography showcasing products on models, lifestyle images, brand videos, and written content telling your story. Quality visuals significantly impact conversion rates and brand perception. Invest in professional photography rather than amateur images - this investment pays dividends in perceived value and sales. Prepare product descriptions, about page content, and marketing copy that communicates your brand voice and value proposition.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Build Pre-Launch Audience</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Start marketing 2-3 months before products arrive. Capture email addresses through a coming soon page. Build social media following with behind-the-scenes content, design process sharing, and brand storytelling. Create anticipation through teaser campaigns and countdowns. Pre-launch audience ensures day-one sales when products become available. Email marketing provides highest ROI - build your list early and nurture relationships before asking for sales.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Plan Launch Campaign</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Design your launch campaign to maximize initial impact. Coordinate website launch, social media announcements, email marketing, and any paid advertising for maximum effect. Consider launch promotions or limited-time offers to drive initial sales. Plan content calendar for launch week and following weeks to maintain momentum. Launch campaigns create urgency and excitement - leverage this energy to drive early sales and reviews.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Establish Ongoing Marketing Channels</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Plan ongoing marketing beyond launch. This may include social media content marketing, email newsletters, influencer partnerships, paid advertising, SEO optimization, or PR outreach. Diversify channels to reduce dependence on any single source. Establish content production workflows to maintain consistent marketing activity. Marketing is not a one-time launch activity - consistent effort builds sustainable growth.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Set Analytics and KPIs</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Implement analytics tracking from launch to measure performance. Track website traffic, conversion rates, customer acquisition costs, average order values, and customer lifetime value. Establish KPIs for marketing channels and overall business performance. Data-driven marketing decisions optimize spending and strategy over time. What you measure improves - establish clear metrics and review performance regularly to guide marketing investments.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 9: Operations and Fulfillment -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Operations and Fulfillment</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Operations infrastructure ensures smooth order fulfillment, customer satisfaction, and business scalability. Behind every successful launch is solid operational capability. Poor operations damage brand reputation through delayed shipments, poor customer service, and fulfillment errors. Invest in operational setup before launch to handle demand professionally from day one.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Choose Fulfillment Model</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Decide between self-fulfillment, third-party logistics (3PL), or dropshipping. Self-fulfillment provides control but requires space and labor. 3PLs handle storage, picking, packing, and shipping for a fee, enabling scalability without operational overhead. Dropshipping eliminates inventory holding but limits quality control and margin. Most successful brands start with self-fulfillment or 3PL rather than dropshipping for quality control. Consider <a href="/oem-clothing-manufacturer">OEM manufacturing</a> for scalable production as you grow.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Set Up Inventory Management</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Establish systems to track inventory levels across SKUs and sizes. Integrate inventory management with your ecommerce platform to prevent overselling. Plan inventory storage whether in your space, warehouse, or 3PL facility. Establish reordering processes to maintain stock availability without overstocking. Inventory management prevents stockouts that lose sales and overstocking that ties up capital. Good inventory systems scale with your business growth. Consider <a href="/private-label-clothing-guide">private label</a> for manageable inventory testing.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Establish Shipping and Packaging</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Set up shipping accounts with carriers like UPS, FedEx, DHL, or regional providers. Negotiate rates based on expected volume. Design packaging that protects products during shipping while reinforcing brand identity. Include branded elements like custom boxes, tissue paper, or thank-you cards. Calculate shipping costs accurately to charge customers appropriately or build into pricing. Fast, reliable shipping significantly impacts customer satisfaction and repeat purchases. Consider <a href="/private-label-clothing-guide">private label options</a> for branded packaging solutions.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Prepare Customer Service Systems</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Establish customer service channels - email, phone, chat, or social media messaging. Set response time expectations and create templates for common inquiries. Prepare policies for returns, exchanges, and refunds. Clear policies build customer trust and reduce conflict. Consider customer service software to manage inquiries efficiently. Excellent customer service differentiates brands and drives repeat business. Plan staffing or allocation of time for customer service from launch. Contact <a href="/contact/">SDF Clothing</a> for production support.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Implement Returns and Exchanges</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Develop clear return and exchange policies that balance customer satisfaction with business viability. Fashion typically experiences 20-30% return rates - plan for this reality. Establish processes for handling returns efficiently including inspection, restocking, and refund processing. Consider third-party returns management services for scale. Easy returns encourage purchases but create operational burden - find the right balance for your brand and customers.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Plan for Growth and Scaling</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Choose operational systems that can scale with your growth. Ecommerce platforms, inventory systems, and fulfillment partners should accommodate increased volume without requiring complete replacement. Document operational processes to enable delegation as you grow. Plan hiring needs for customer service, fulfillment, and operations as volume increases. Scalable operations prevent operational crises when demand increases. Consider <a href="/oem-clothing-manufacturer">OEM manufacturing</a> for scalable custom production.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

